            <ArticleTitle>[Evaluation of amlodipine in stable effort angina in office cardiologic practice].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>489-96</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A multicentre trial was undertaken in order to evaluate the therapeutic efficacy and ease of use of amlopidine in 1890 patients with stable effort angina. Involving prescribing conditions close to those of everyday practice, it revealed an improvement in angina symptomatology with one daily dose of amlopidine. After one month's treatment, 49.8% of patients were able to reduce or stop their use of fast-acting nitrites. After three months' treatment with amlopidine, 51% of patients had become asymptomatic. This study also revealed a decrease in the duration and severity of angina attacks. Safety/acceptability of amlopidine, assessed by clinical and laboratory parameters, was good, adverse events (22.7%), most often benign, improving or disappearing in more than half of all cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
